// A declaration that says a destructor inverts its constructor mints an η cell.
//
// `unwrap` takes a `Wrap` apart and `MkWrap` puts one together, and the rule
// below says so: `unwrap(MkWrap(x))` is `x`. That is one half of an
// isomorphism. The other half — rebuilding what you just took apart gets you
// back where you started — is the η law, and the cell layer mints it from the
// declaration rather than waiting for it to be written:
//
//     ⟨w |+ unwrap(; MkWrap⁻(β))⟩  ~>  ⟨w |+ β⟩
//
// Read left to right: destruct `w` with `unwrap`, hand the result to
// `MkWrap⁻`, which rebuilds it, and pass that on — the same as passing `w` on
// untouched. The cell is written in the contracting direction only. Its mirror
// would rewrite *any* cut of the right polarity into an `unwrap`/`MkWrap` pair,
// whatever the type of the thing being cut, so it is not minted.
//
// The declaration licenses the law by stating both halves, and both are
// checked. One constructor, because the cancellation is false at every other
// constructor of a type that has several. And the rule below, because without
// it `unwrap` is an arbitrary operation and there is nothing to cancel against.
//
// Three cells reach the store: `MkWrap`'s return-side frame cell, the written
// rule, and the η cell. Data η is a call-by-value law, so the cell is minted at
// a positive cut and refuses to fire at a negative one.
data Wrap : Type {
  MkWrap : (n : Wrap) --> Wrap;
  oper unwrap(w : Wrap) -> Wrap;
  rule unwrap(MkWrap(x)) ==> x;
}

//@ mode: desc
//@ expect-desc-rules: 1
//@ expect-desc-store-cells: 3
//@ expect-desc-eta-cells: 1
